Add a Database to Your App in 6 Minutes
Video 3 Script (teleprompter-ready, same style as video 1)

Today I’m going to show you how to add a real database to the app we built.
In about six minutes.
No theory.
No database lecture.
Just execution.

We are going to make this app feel real.

Here is what we are going to do.
We will create a Supabase project.
We will add environment variables to our app.
We will write one simple query.
We will display real data on a page.
Then we commit.
Push.
And redeploy.

The tools we are using are the same as before.
GitHub.
Vercel.
Antigravity.
And now Supabase for the database.

I assume you already have Node installed.
You have a GitHub account.
You have a Vercel account.
You have Antigravity installed.
And you have the project from the previous videos running.

My name is Steve.
I was a professional software tester for over 12 years.
My job was to find where systems break and prove where they remain stable.
So we are not guessing here.
We are building this in a controlled way.

Let’s start.

First we go to Supabase.
We create a new project.
Give it a name.
Choose a region close to you.
Set a database password.
And wait a minute while it provisions.

That’s it.
No deep database talk.
It’s just infrastructure handled for you.

Once the project is ready, we go to the project settings.
We copy the project URL.
And we copy the anon public key.

Now we go back to our app.

We create a local environment file.
Dot env local.
And we add two variables.
The Supabase URL.
And the Supabase anon key.

That’s it.
No magic.

Now we install the Supabase client in the project.
One command.
Nothing fancy.

Next step.
We create a simple table in Supabase.
Let’s call it messages.
One column called id.
One column called content.
We insert one or two rows manually.

Now we go back to our app.

We create a small helper file to initialize Supabase using the environment variables.
Just basic setup.
Nothing advanced.

Now we add a simple query.
Select everything from messages.
That’s it.

We place that query inside a page.
Or inside a server component.
And we render the results on the screen.

No architecture debates.
No patterns discussion.
Just get the data.
Show the data.

Now we run the app locally.

We refresh the page.
And if everything is correct, we see real data coming from a real database.

This is the moment where it feels different.

Before, it was a template.
Now it is connected to a database.
Now it behaves like an actual application.

Now we commit.

Clear message.
Add Supabase integration and messages query.

We push to GitHub.

Vercel detects the change.
Triggers a new build.
And redeploys automatically.

But we are not done yet.

We also need to add the same environment variables in Vercel.
So we go to the project settings in Vercel.
Add the Supabase URL.
Add the Supabase anon key.
Save.
Redeploy if needed.

Now we open the live site.

We refresh.

And we see database data rendered from production.

That’s it.

You just connected a real database.
Without configuring a server.
Without setting up Docker.
Without managing infrastructure.

This is the baseline.

You deploy.
You connect data.
You display it.
You redeploy.

Now it feels real.

In the next video, we’ll look at what usually breaks when you introduce a database.
And how to prevent those mistakes before they cost you time.